# DSL ↔ TLA+ cheatsheet

Every `Notary.DSL` form next to the TLA+ it generates. For the walkthrough,
see [Writing specs without learning TLA+](dsl.md). The generated `.tla` in
`specs/` is always the source of truth: when in doubt, run
`mix notary.compile` and read it.

## Module
{: .col-2}

### The spec module

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Specs.Checkout do
  use Notary.DSL, name: "Checkout"
  description "A three-step checkout."
  # ...
end
```

```text
---- MODULE Checkout ----
\* A three-step checkout.

EXTENDS Naturals
...
=====
```

Writes `specs/Checkout.tla` and `specs/Checkout.cfg`. `name:` defaults to
the module's last segment. `EXTENDS` is chosen for you: `Integers` replaces
`Naturals` when the spec has negative numbers, and `Sequences` and
`FiniteSets` are added only when the spec uses them.

### Constants

```elixir
constant max: 3
```

```text
\* .tla
CONSTANT max

\* .cfg
CONSTANT max = 3
```

Use the name (`max`) anywhere an expression goes. Keep model values small.

### Raw TLA+

```elixir
raw ~S"""
Twice(x) == x + x
"""
```

```text
Twice(x) == x + x
```

Pasted in before `Next`, for anything the DSL can't express. `~S` keeps
TLA+'s backslashes literal; a plain string works too.

## State
{: .col-2}

### Variables and types

| DSL | TLA+ (`TypeOK`) |
|---|---|
| `variable flag: boolean()` | `flag \in BOOLEAN` |
| `variable n: 0..max` | `n \in 0..max` |
| `variable step: ["a", "b"]` | `step \in {"a", "b"}` |
| `variable r: %{ok: boolean()}` | `r \in [ok: BOOLEAN]` |

Each `variable` adds to `VARIABLES` and one conjunct to `TypeOK`, which is
checked as an invariant automatically.

### Initial state

```elixir
initial flag: false, n: 0, step: "a"
```

```text
Init == /\ flag = FALSE
        /\ n = 0
        /\ step = "a"
```

Must give every variable a value.

## Actions
{: .col-2}

### Guard and assign

```elixir
action Greet do
  guard greeted == false
  assign greeted: true
end
```

```text
Greet == /\ greeted = FALSE
         /\ greeted' = TRUE

Next == \/ Greet
```

Each `guard` is one conjunct. `assign(a: 1, b: 2)` gives one `x' = ...`
line per variable. Every action becomes a disjunct of `Next`.

### What stays the same

```elixir
action Inc do
  assign n: n + 1
end
```

```text
Inc == /\ n' = n + 1
       /\ UNCHANGED <<flag, step>>
```

Variables an action doesn't assign get `UNCHANGED` automatically.

### Explicit UNCHANGED

```elixir
action Inc, explicit_unchanged: true do
  assign n: n + 1
  unchanged [flag, step]
end
```

```text
Inc == /\ n' = n + 1
       /\ UNCHANGED <<flag, step>>
```

`unchanged flag` covers one variable; `unchanged [:flag, :step]` works too.

With `explicit_unchanged: true`, a variable you don't cover is a compile
error.

### Parameters

```elixir
action Deposit, amount: 1..2 do
  guard n + amount <= max
  assign n: n + amount
end
```

```text
Deposit(amount) == /\ (n + amount) <= max
                   /\ n' = n + amount
                   /\ UNCHANGED <<...>>

Next == \/ \E amount \in 1..2 : Deposit(amount)
```

The domain (a range or a list of values) is also what conformance tests
generate inputs from.

### Doc comments

```elixir
action Deposit, amount: 1..2, doc: "Add money." do
```

```text
\* Add money.
Deposit(amount) == ...
```

### Both and either

| DSL | TLA+ |
|---|---|
| `both(assign(a: 1), assign(b: 2))` | `a' = 1 /\ b' = 2` |
| `either(assign(a: 1), assign(b: 2))` | `(a' = 1 /\ UNCHANGED b) \/ (b' = 2 /\ UNCHANGED a)` |
| `parallel do ... end` | each line conjoined with `/\` |

`either` is a nondeterministic choice: TLC explores both branches. Each
branch keeps the variables only its sibling assigns `UNCHANGED`.

## Expressions
{: .col-2}

### Values and logic

| DSL | TLA+ |
|---|---|
| `1`, `"deposit"` | `1`, `"deposit"` |
| `true`, `false` | `TRUE`, `FALSE` |
| `count` (a variable) | `count` |
| `a and b` | `a /\ b` |
| `a or b` | `a \/ b` |
| `not a` | `~a` |
| `if c, do: a, else: b` | `IF c THEN a ELSE b` |
| `unless c, do: a, else: b` | `IF ~c THEN a ELSE b` |

Every `if`/`unless` needs an `else`. `cond`, `case`, `with`, `for` and
`fn` aren't supported: nest `if`s or use `raw`.

### Arithmetic and comparison

| DSL | TLA+ |
|---|---|
| `a + b`, `a - b`, `a * b` | `a + b`, `a - b`, `a * b` |
| `a / b` | `a \div b` (integer division) |
| `rem(a, b)` | `a \mod b` |
| `a == b`, `a != b` | `a = b`, `a # b` |
| `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` | `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` |
| `min(a, b)` | `IF a <= b THEN a ELSE b` |
| `max(a, b)` | `IF a >= b THEN a ELSE b` |
| `-a`, `-1` | `-a`, `-1` (switches `EXTENDS` to `Integers`) |

An assigned comparison is parenthesized: `assign(flag: n == 0)` →
`flag' = (n = 0)`.

### Sets

| DSL | TLA+ |
|---|---|
| `set([1, 2])` | `{1, 2}` |
| `1..3` | `1..3` |
| `x in s`, `member?(x, s)` | `x \in s` |
| `x in ["a", "b"]` | `x \in {"a", "b"}` |
| `x not in s` | `x \notin s` |
| `set_union(a, b)` | `a \cup b` |
| `set_intersect(a, b)` | `a \cap b` |
| `set_diff(a, b)` | `a \ b` |
| `cardinality(s)` | `Cardinality(s)` |

A list literal on the right of `in` (or `member?`) is a set. Anywhere else
a list is a sequence (below): use `set([...])` for a set value.

### Sequences

| DSL | TLA+ |
|---|---|
| `[a, b]`, `{a, b}` | `<<a, b>>` |
| `head(s)`, `tail(s)` | `Head(s)`, `Tail(s)` |
| `length(s)` | `Len(s)` |
| `append(s, x)` | `s \o <<x>>` |
| `concat(a, b)` | `a \o b` |
| `at(s, i)` | `s[i]` |
| `subseq(s, m, n)` | `SubSeq(s, m, n)` |

Indexes start at 1, as in TLA+.

### Records

```elixir
%{a: 1}          # or [a: 1], or record(a: 1)
domain(r)
```

```text
[a |-> 1]
DOMAIN(r)
```

Reading a field (`r.a`) isn't supported yet: use `raw`.

## Checking
{: .col-2}

### Invariants

```elixir
invariant Bounded, doc: "n stays small",
  do: n <= max
```

```text
\* n stays small
Bounded ==
n <= max

\* .cfg
INVARIANT TypeOK Bounded
```

Names must be CamelCase. `TypeOK` comes from the variable types and is
always checked.

### Temporal properties

| DSL | TLA+ |
|---|---|
| `property P, do: always(n >= 0)` | `P == [](n >= 0)` |
| `property P, do: eventually(done)` | `P == <>done` |
| `property P, do: leads_to(a, b)` | `P == a ~> b` |
| `enabled(Pay)` | `ENABLED <<Pay>>_<<vars>>` |
| `enabled(:Deposit, [1])` | `ENABLED <<Deposit(1)>>_<<vars>>` |

Each property is listed under `PROPERTY` in the `.cfg`. In `ENABLED`, `<<vars>>`
stands for the tuple of all the spec's variables.

### Fairness

| DSL | TLA+ (in `Spec`) |
|---|---|
| `fair Pay` | `WF_vars(Pay)` |
| `strong_fair [Pay, Ship]` | `SF_vars(Pay) /\ SF_vars(Ship)` |
| `fair Deposit` (with `amount: 1..2`) | `\A amount \in 1..2 : WF_vars(Deposit(amount))` |

Needed for "eventually" properties: without it, TLC may let the system
stop forever.

### Deadlock

When every action has a guard, the `.cfg` gets `CHECK_DEADLOCK FALSE`: a
state where nothing is enabled is treated as a finished run, not an error.

